# Nevermined Router — paying external services

You are writing an agent that must **pay** an external service it has no account with — any x402
agent or MPP merchant — through the Nevermined Router. If you are *receiving* payments or buying a
Nevermined plan, use the `nevermined-payments` rules instead.

**The workflow — set up, discover, quote, pay, verify — is the buyer skill:
https://nevermined.app/catalog/skill.md** (also on GitHub, with its `references/`:
https://github.com/nevermined-io/docs/tree/main/skills/nevermined-router). Follow it for the steps.
This file condenses them for code that calls the Router, with the guardrails and gotchas to keep.
Nevermined credits (pay catalog services with credits instead of a Delegation; free at sign-up, from top-up packs or from a Nevermined Subscription): https://nevermined.ai/docs/products/catalog/router/subscription
Docs: https://nevermined.ai/docs/products/catalog/router/overview

## When the Router does NOT apply

It pays a price quoted **on the wire** for one request. A conventional SaaS API (Exa, Firecrawl,
Tavily…) is billed out of band by a monthly plan and a long-lived key, so it never quotes a
per-request price — there is nothing to pay and no address to pay it to.

**A service answering `401`/`403` rather than `402` wants authentication, not payment.** Say so;
don't try to route it.

## Environment

Live by default: real merchants and real money, within the cap a human approves.

- `NVM_API_URL` — `https://api.live.nevermined.app`. To test first with testnet funds, a `sandbox:`
  key uses `https://api.sandbox.nevermined.app` instead (Catalog MCP:
  `https://mcp.sandbox.nevermined.app/mcp`). A key works only on the deployment that issued it, and
  the sandbox Router funds testnets only, so of its listings only the two free testnet test
  merchants can be paid there.
- `NVM_API_KEY` — the whole key, prefix included: `live:…` (`sandbox:…` on the sandbox). **Never
  send it to the merchant**; it goes to the Router only, which does not forward your
  `Authorization` header. The merchant's own auth goes in `headers` on `/route`, or in
  `X-Router-Upstream-Authorization` on `/svc`·`/proxy`.
- `NVM_DELEGATION_ID` — the spending Delegation

## 1. Create a Delegation (the budget)

```bash
curl -sX POST "$NVM_API_URL/api/v1/delegation/create" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NVM_API_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"provider":"erc4337","currency":"usdc","spendingLimitCents":500,"durationSecs":604800}'
```

Set `spendingLimitCents` and `durationSecs` to the budget and duration the human gave you — the
`500` ($5.00) and 7 days above are only an example — and reuse an Active Delegation
(`GET /api/v1/delegation?accessible=true`) instead of creating one per call.

All four fields are required — no defaults. `erc4337` is the crypto-funded Delegation both
stablecoin rails need. Two guards refuse this call outright, neither retryable:

- `403 BCK.OAUTH.0030` — the key was OAuth-minted. It may not create Delegations or use
  `/router/{payments,route,quote,select,proxy,svc}`. Use a plain account-owner key — or, for a `commerce` grant,
  spend through `POST /router/commerce/route` and price through `/router/commerce/quote`, which
  derive the Delegation from the grant.
- `412 {"error":"consent_required","outdated":[…]}` — the account's legal consent lapsed; a human
  must accept. ⚠️ Its only `code` is the generic `BCK.HTTP.412`, so branch on `body.error`.

## 2. Fund the buyer wallet

Both rails **pull** from your own wallet: a Delegation authorizes a spend, it does not
supply funds. Read the address to fund off the live Delegation every time:

```bash
curl -s "$NVM_API_URL/api/v1/delegation/$NVM_DELEGATION_ID?withPayer=true" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NVM_API_KEY"
# fundingWalletAddress is the wallet to fund (not providerPaymentMethodId)
```

`fundingWalletAddress` is the wallet the Router spends from, and only `?withPayer=true` returns it.
For a personal Delegation it is your own wallet, not `providerPaymentMethodId` (the smart-account
address), which the Router never spends from; for an org-wallet Delegation the two are the same.
`null` on a crypto Delegation means the wallet could not be resolved: read it again, and if it stays
`null`, stop and report it to the human. Never fund `providerPaymentMethodId` instead.

**Never reuse a cached address.** A stale one is the most common cause of an underfunded wallet. On
MPP `tempo` that is `402 BCK.ROUTER.0009`, refused before anything is signed; its message names the
wallet and chain it checked, so compare them with `fundingWalletAddress`. x402 and MPP `evm` are
not balance-checked, so there the payment fails at settlement instead. MPP `evm` (USDC on Base) pays
from an organization wallet only: a personal wallet pays over the service's x402 offer, or is
refused `0001` when it has none.

**One x402 network is funded per deployment, fixed by its environment: sandbox → `base-sepolia`,
live → `base`.** A merchant on the other chain is unpayable from where you are and fails
`400 BCK.ROUTER.0001 … no fundable option` — which reads like a broken service and is not.

## 3. Discover (public, no API key)

```bash
curl -s https://nevermined.app/catalog/ai-catalog.json \
  | jq '[.services[] | select(.protocol == "x402" and .category == "Search & Research") | {slug, title, priceLabel}]'
```

- One JSON feed of every listed service — filter it locally. `/api/v1/catalog/services` and
  `/api/v1/catalog/categories` are **not a public API**: they return `403` by design. For server-side
  search, use the Catalog MCP (`search_services`, `get_service`, `list_categories`) at
  `https://mcp.live.nevermined.app/mcp` — free, no key.
- **Only `protocol` of `x402` or `mpp` is routable.** Filter for them.
- **Pay a listed service by its `slug`, never by URL** — the feed has no merchant URL, and a raw-URL
  payment to a cataloged host is refused (`409 BCK.ROUTER.0014`). The subpath to send is the
  endpoint's `invokePath` when present — even `""`, which means "append nothing" — else its `path`:

```ts
const subpath = endpoint.invokePath ?? endpoint.path // NOT || — '' must stay '' or the path double-stacks
```

- `category` is a **closed 13-value enum** (`"Search & Research"`, not `"Search"`), with a free-text
  `subCategory` under it. A misspelt one filters to an empty list, not an error — read the values in
  use from the feed (`group_by(.category)`), never guess the string.

## 4. Quote, then pay

Price the exact request for free, then pay it. A listed service is paid by `slug`, two ways:

- `ALL /api/v1/router/svc/<slug>/<invokePath>[?query]` — the buyer skill's path: the endpoint's
  method, query and JSON body, with `Authorization`, `X-Router-Delegation-Id` and
  `X-Router-Request-Id` headers and no `X-Router-Target-Slug` (`400 BCK.ROUTER.0001`). **It takes no
  per-call price ceiling**: the Delegation's cap and expiry bound what it can spend, so keep the cap
  small.
- `POST /api/v1/router/route` — JSON in, JSON out, and `maxTotalCents`, the per-call ceiling. To
  refuse a call that costs more than its quote, pay here:

```ts
const API = `${process.env.NVM_API_URL}/api/v1/router`
const headers = {
  Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.NVM_API_KEY}`,
  'Content-Type': 'application/json',
}
const call = {
  delegationId: process.env.NVM_DELEGATION_ID,
  slug: '<slug>', // from the catalog
  path: '<invokePath>', // the endpoint's invokePath (else its path)
  method: 'POST',
  body: { query: '…' }, // filled from the endpoint's request schema
}

// Free: nothing signed or reserved, but the service is sent the unpaid request
const quote = await (await fetch(`${API}/quote`, { method: 'POST', headers, body: JSON.stringify(call) })).json()
if (quote.upstreamStatus !== 402) throw new Error(`not a priced call: upstream ${quote.upstreamStatus}`)

const res = await fetch(`${API}/route`, {
  method: 'POST',
  headers,
  body: JSON.stringify({
    ...call,
    maxTotalCents: Number(quote.fee.capChargedCents), // dearer now → 402 BCK.ROUTER.0018, not paid
    requestId: 'search-nevermined-router-v1', // stable per PURCHASE, not per attempt
  }),
})
const { status, body, paid, payment } = await res.json()
```

The Router probes, auto-detects the protocol from the 402, pays and relays. `status`/`body` are the
merchant's own; `paid: false` with no `payment` means it was free — handle that. An off-catalog
service takes `url` in place of `slug`/`path`. For streaming to a URL use
`ALL /api/v1/router/proxy` with `X-Router-Target-Url`, `X-Router-Delegation-Id` and
`X-Router-Request-Id` headers.

**The quote: `POST /api/v1/router/quote`** (API 1.48+) — the same body minus
`requestId`, `maxTotalCents` and `protocol`, which are stripped, not refused (`delegationId` optional). Nothing is signed, minted, recorded or reserved, but the Router **does**
send the unpaid request to the service (a service that does not charge for it performs it), and a
quote spends the same per-key and per-service rate budgets as a payment — quote once per decision,
don't poll. Pay with `maxTotalCents` set to its `fee.capChargedCents` (whole cents rounded **up**
from `fee.capChargedMicros`), so a price that rose in between is refused (`402 BCK.ROUTER.0018`)
instead of paid; any price up to that whole cent is still paid. It checks neither your cap nor your
wallet. A quote whose `settlement.scheme` is `session` also states `sessionDeposit`: that deposit
leaves the wallet at the first call, however small, and `maxTotalCents` does not bound it. Read `upstreamStatus`: `402` is priced; a `2xx` asked no payment and the quote already ran
it once, so there is nothing to pay; `401`/`403` wants the service's own login; `429` or a `5xx`,
try later; any other `4xx`, fix the request before you pay. Skip the quote for an endpoint whose
`access` is `free` or `free-after-purchase`: a quote runs it, so call it instead. If such a call
asks for payment anyway, the Router pays it with no per-call ceiling; on `/route`, send
`maxTotalCents: 0` to have it refused (`0018`) instead. An OAuth
`commerce` key quotes on `POST /router/commerce/quote` (no `delegationId`; first API release after 1.49, `404` until then).

API 1.55+: a payment-required quote returns `quoteId` + `expiresAt` (60 s). Pass the id with the exact unchanged
`/route` call; it binds the target, request, Delegation, rail and exact total. Re-quote if it expires
or the call changes. `/router/select` and MCP `route_by_intent` also accept exact slugs in
`filters.require|prefer|exclude`; `require` selects that slug or fails closed, without fallback.

### `requestId` is an idempotency key, not a request counter

**A fresh `uuid4()` per HTTP attempt is how an agent double-spends.** Use one stable id per logical
purchase, reused across retries of it: the same id returns the original payment, a fresh id buys
again. Derive it from the work being done.

**From API 1.48, `202 { paymentId, resultUrl, status: "Pending" }` means PAID and still running** (the
service took over 45 s). Poll `GET $NVM_API_URL{resultUrl}` until `state` is `Ready` or `Failed`; never
re-buy it with a fresh `requestId` — a same-id retry returns that `202`, then the retained result.

### Money arithmetic

Budget is debited in **whole cents, rounded up** — 1000 calls at $0.001 costs **$10.00, not $1.00**.
`settlement.approxCents` is only the **merchant** leg; Nevermined's routing fee rides on top in the
always-present `payment.fee` (`{ bps, amount, cents, capChargedCents, capChargedMicros }`). **`fee.capChargedCents` is
what the call reserved against your cap**, rounded up to a whole cent (from API 1.48 the exact reserve is
`capChargedMicros`, in 1/10,000 of a cent) — sum that, not `approxCents`, or your accounting
under-reports by exactly the fee. ⚠️ It is the reserve **at mint**: a mode-B hop that does not return
`2xx` releases the fee half back, so a running total over-reports on those calls. For spend to date
read `GET /api/v1/delegation/{id}` → `amountSpentCents`.

## Guardrails — a refusal is the system working

- `BCK.ROUTER.0003` (402) — Delegation over cap, expired, exhausted or revoked. **Stop.**
- `BCK.ROUTER.0009` (402) — an MPP `tempo` payment found the wallet short on its Tempo chain, or the card was declined (MPP-stripe); nothing was signed. **Stop.** x402 and MPP `evm` are never balance-checked: a short wallet still gets its credential and fails at settlement.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0002` (409) — `requestId` already used; the original `paymentId` is in the response.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0001` (400) — bad input / no fundable option / non-allowlisted asset; `details` names it.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0008` (403) — legacy API key; create a new one.
- Only `BCK.ROUTER.0006` (500, summary read), `0007` (429, too many concurrent), `0020` (5xx/429), `0022` (500, required capability unavailable: service selection, buyer identity signing or partner-edge token signing;
  retry only after operator repair, never loop unchanged), `0028` (503, quote), `0042` (409, closing payment channel), `0043` (503, busy payment channel), `0048` (503, Privy did not answer), `0051` (409, closest catalog match unavailable), `0052` (503, autoPay while semantic ranking is unavailable), `0057` (503, Permit2 approval did not complete) and `0061` (500, a credits call to a partner service could not be signed after its payment was recorded, which is refunded; retry only after operator repair, with a new `requestId`, never loop unchanged) are **retryable**, and so are `BCK.X402.0052` (503, wallet upgrade did not complete) and `BCK.USER_PROFILE.0006` (503, wallet-ownership check unanswered) — on the paying path, `0007`, `0020`, `0042`, `0043`, `0048`, `0051`, `0052`, `0057`, `BCK.X402.0052` and `BCK.USER_PROFILE.0006`. Everything else is a decision — retrying
  unchanged gives the same answer.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0010` (500) — internal. **Never blind-retry it:** a credential was already minted and
  no record was written, so `requestId` will not suppress the retry. Report it.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0011` (402) — card rail: needs cardholder 3-D Secure, which an agent can't complete.
  Nothing was charged. **Don't auto-retry** — each attempt re-demands 3DS and strands a fresh
  single-use card credential. Surface it to a human.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0012` (400) — the seller's 402 advertises an EIP-712 domain its own settlement
  token does not sign under. Nothing signed, charged or reserved. It is the seller's bug and a
  retry gives the same answer: report it and pay someone else.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0013` (500) — Nevermined holds no EIP-712 domain for the selected token: OUR
  table is incomplete, not the seller's bug. Nothing signed, charged or reserved. Report it
  to Nevermined; a retry fails identically.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0014` (409) — the target is a cataloged Nevermined service, whose upstream URL is
  deliberately hidden; the Router will not pay it by raw URL (mode A or a raw mode-B target puts
  the merchant's host on your wire). A retry with the same raw URL fails identically — invoke it
  through the broker: `POST /router/route` with a `slug`, or `POST /router/svc/<catalog-slug>`
  (a `commerce` grant: `POST /router/commerce/route`). A refused **quote** is re-quoted by slug on
  `/router/quote` or `/router/commerce/quote`, never paid.
  The match is by HOST, so a co-hosted endpoint that is not itself listed is refused too — ask the vendor to list it, or contact Nevermined; hosts with no cataloged service are unaffected.

- `BCK.ROUTER.0018` (402) — `maxTotalCents` is below the fee-inclusive, rounded reserve.
  No charge or cap debit, though signing may already have occurred. Parse JSON-string `params` for `requiredTotalCents`; only raise the ceiling if this call is intended.
  Reuse the same `requestId` after a refusal. Do not auto-retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0019` (4xx) — (streaming `/proxy`·`/svc` only) a cataloged service rejected the request (a 4xx, or a rare 3xx the Router does not follow; not a 402/429). The upstream body is withheld as a host oracle; the typed error preserves the real status. Fix the request from the service's Catalog detail — not retryable.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0020` (5xx / 429) — (streaming `/proxy`·`/svc` only) a cataloged service errored or rate-limited (upstream/transient). Body and headers are withheld; the real status is preserved. The Router charges no routing fee for an undelivered call; retry with backoff and the **same** `requestId`: the Router never pays one id twice. If an `X-Router-Payment-Id` came back, a payment is recorded: look it up by your `requestId` (`GET /router/payments?requestId=`) first. A fresh id buys again from a merchant that may already have been paid: the error's own `hint` calls that a NEW `requestId` for a fresh attempt, so make it only after the lookup, as a deliberate second purchase.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0021` (400) — the payment credential would overwrite a header you asked the Router to forward: the MPP rails carry it in `Authorization`, which is also where your own merchant auth goes. Nothing was minted and no money moved. Name the header the service documents for its credential — `credentialHeader` in the `/route` body, or `X-Router-Credential-Header` on `/proxy`·`/svc` (a separate `Payment` header is the common one) — and your `Authorization` is left untouched. Do not retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0024` (413) — the request body exceeds the Router's size limit (about 5 MB). Do not retry unchanged; reduce the request body.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0025` (502) — reply too large after a paid request; payment may have gone through. The reply has no `X-Router-Payment-Id`; JSON-string `params` may carry `paymentId`. If `params` has no `paymentId`, look the payment up by your `requestId`: `GET /api/v1/router/payments?requestId=<id>` (percent-encoded) answers `{ presence, payments }`. A 0025 always records the payment first, so `absent` there means you queried the wrong id, account or environment — never that nothing was charged. The row reads `status: Failed` because delivery failed, not because the merchant was uncharged. A non-null `merchantSettlementObservedAt` confirms the merchant was paid on-chain; null does not prove no charge on any rail. Do not retry: the same `requestId` returns 409 `BCK.ROUTER.0002` with the original `paymentId` and cannot re-deliver the reply; a fresh id risks another charge.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0026` (415) — the Router cannot forward this request body. The streaming surfaces (`/router/svc/:slug`, `/router/proxy`) forward only JSON or URL-encoded bodies; any other type (`multipart/form-data`, `text/plain`, `application/octet-stream`, a vendor `+json`) or any body on GET/HEAD is refused. No payment was minted and no money moved. Resend as JSON or a URL-encoded form; retrying unchanged fails identically.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0027` (413) — the request body is larger than the catalog endpoint accepts (`maxRequestBytes` on the service detail / MCP `get_service`; Locus gateways take 8,000 bytes). Refused before the service is contacted: no payment was minted and no money moved. `params` carries `bodyBytes` and `maxRequestBytes`. Shrink the body or pick a service that takes it; do not retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0028` (503) — `POST /router/quote` could not price the call because a dependent read failed (e.g. settlement-token details). Nothing is signed or charged on the quote path; retry with backoff. The same condition would also fail a payment, so do not route the call meanwhile.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0029` (404) — invalid or wrong-account `quoteId`; nothing signed, reserved or charged. Request a new quote and use its id; do not retry the invalid id.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0031` (409) — the exact slug in `filters.require` cannot be selected. Inspect `params.reason`; correct the slug/request, or deliberately remove `require` to allow fallback. Do not retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0032` (410) — the quote expired before payment; nothing signed, reserved or charged. Quote again and decide against the new total; do not retry the expired id.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0033` (409) — the payment differs from the quote's target, method, headers, body, credential header, delegation, rail, or exact total. Send the quoted call unchanged or request a new quote; do not retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0034` (502) — a paid request got no response (timeout or connection failure); the payment may have gone through. Look the payment up by your `requestId`: `GET /api/v1/router/payments?requestId=<your requestId>` (percent-encoded; the `paymentId` in `params` is not a lookup key), then reconcile its `status` and `merchantSettlementObservedAt`. Never retry with a fresh `requestId`.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0035` (422) — the service's challenge asks for no payment (an auth-only wallet sign-in) and the Router could not answer it. It signs one only on a slug call to an `auth: "siwx"` endpoint (an async result poll) or follow-up path, after your own wallet paid that service within the follow-up window. Nothing was charged; pay first from your own wallet and poll through the same slug, or authenticate with the service directly. Do not retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0036` (422) — the endpoint is known to outlast the Router's 120 s paid-call limit, so paying would charge and deliver nothing; refused before payment. `params.source` is `declared` (permanent) or `observed` (lifts at `params.liftsAt`). Pick another endpoint; do not retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0040` (500) — internal: the Router's MPP session channel state refused an inconsistent read or write. Nothing moved. Report it; do not retry.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0041` (400) — the service's MPP challenge offers only a session, which `POST /router/payments` cannot pay. There it is the answer for every crypto delegation, even one with `allowSessionPayments: false` (the Router-fetched routes answer that one with `0047` unless they can pay the service another way). Use `/router/route`, `/router/svc/<slug>` or `/router/proxy`, or, if the service also offers x402, pay that offer with `protocol: "x402"`.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0042` (409) — the MPP session payment channel with this service is closing; no payment signed or charged. Retry later with the same `requestId`.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0043` (503) — the MPP session payment channel with this service is busy; nothing signed or charged. Retry after a few seconds with the same `requestId`.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0044` (422) — the Router cannot collect its fee on this MPP session payment: `params.cause` is `org-wallet` (pay from a delegation funded by your own wallet) or `network` (session fees are not collected on that network; pay another network's service). Nothing charged. Do not retry unchanged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0045` (409) — the service does not offer the rail pinned by `requireProtocol` (`X-Router-Require-Protocol` on `/proxy` · `/svc`; x402 or mpp). Nothing signed, reserved or charged. Not retryable unchanged: choose a service on your required rail, or deliberately change the pin; never drop it automatically.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0046` (400) — you opted in to the body gate and the request body provably fails the endpoint's published request schema; the Router refused before anything was signed, reserved or charged (`params.endpointPath`). Without the opt-in the call is never refused on this basis. The JSON surfaces read the `validateRequestBody` body field: `/route`, `/commerce/route` (and their `/with-controls` variants) and `/router/subscription/route`, and the Commerce MCP `pay_service` forwards it to them. The streaming `/proxy` and `/svc/:slug` surfaces (`/router/subscription/svc/:slug` included) read the `X-Router-Validate-Request-Body: true` header instead. Not retryable unchanged: fix the body to match the endpoint's published schema/example (catalog `requestSchema`/`requestExample`, or the Commerce MCP `get_service`), or drop `validateRequestBody` (or the header).
- `BCK.ROUTER.0047` (409) — this delegation was created with `allowSessionPayments: false` and the Router would pay the service over an MPP session (a refundable deposit leaves the wallet at the first call). Nothing signed, reserved or charged. Raised by the routes and quotes that can pay a session, never by `POST /router/payments`, which answers `0041` instead. Not retryable unchanged: use a service that charges per call, or report to the human, who can deliberately drop a `requireProtocol: "mpp"` pin or create a delegation that allows session payments. An app connected over OAuth holds its delegation through its grant, so they disconnect the app in Nevermined and reconnect it with session payments allowed instead.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0048` (503) — on an MPP `tempo` payment, Privy did not answer when the Router read the wallet's public key (once per wallet). Transient; nothing signed, reserved or charged. Retry after a backoff with the same `requestId`.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0049` (422) — `POST /router/select` or `/router/commerce/select` (MCP `route_by_intent`): no payable candidate's semantic score reached the relevance floor (a service not yet indexed for that score can win on a keyword match; while semantic ranking is unavailable the floor applies to none, and an `autoPay` pick gets `0052`; the floor applies to `filters.prefer` too), or no catalog service matched at all, so it chose none. Nothing charged and no payment attempted, even with `autoPay: true`. JSON-string `params` carries `minSemanticScore` and, unless nothing matched at all, `bestSemanticScore`. When a close enough match is unhealthy or held by moderation right now, the answer is `0051` instead; when no close match returns every medium in `wantedMedia`, `0053`. Not retryable unchanged: rephrase `intent` to name the capability concretely (what you send, what you get back), broaden it or relax `filters`; or search the catalog (`POST /api/v1/ard/search`, MCP `search_services`) and pay one by slug; or pin a slug in `filters.require`, which the floor does not apply to.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0051` (409) — `POST /router/select` or `/router/commerce/select` (MCP `route_by_intent`): a catalog service close enough to the intent is unhealthy or held by moderation right now, and no other payable service matched closely enough, so it chose none. Nothing charged and no payment attempted, even with `autoPay: true`. The cause is on the catalog side, so rephrasing finds the same service: retry the same request later (same `requestId`), or search the catalog (`POST /api/v1/ard/search`, MCP `search_services`) and pay one by slug.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0052` (503) — `POST /router/select` or `/router/commerce/select` with `autoPay: true`: semantic ranking is temporarily unavailable, so the Router will not pay a pick it could only make on keywords. Nothing charged and no payment attempted; a `filters.require` pin is still paid. Retry later with the same `requestId`, or select without `autoPay`, check the pick and pay it yourself by slug.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0053` (422) — `POST /router/select` or `/router/commerce/select` (MCP `route_by_intent`): catalog services matched the intent closely enough, but none is recorded as returning every medium named in `wantedMedia` (image, audio or video; `audio` beside `video` is not checked separately; applies to `filters.prefer` too), so it chose none. Nothing charged and no payment attempted, even with `autoPay: true`. JSON-string `params` carries `wantedMedia` as applied (each medium once, sorted, no `audio` beside `video`). Distinct from `0049`, where nothing matched the intent closely enough. Not retryable unchanged: search the catalog (`POST /api/v1/ard/search`, MCP `search_services`) and pay one by slug, or pin a slug in `filters.require`, which this check does not apply to; name only the media you actually need back.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0054` (422) — `requestId` / `Idempotency-Key` already used for a different request (method, path, query, body or target); nothing paid for it. Retrying an unknown outcome: reconcile with `GET /api/v1/router/payments?requestId=<original>` before a new key, which pays again.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0055` (402) — `POST /router/run`: nothing to pay with; send a `delegationId` (payment methods page). Nothing sent to the merchant or charged.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0056` (422) — the service charges by usage (x402 `upto`): the Router authorizes a maximum through Permit2 and the merchant takes only what the call used, which needs a one-time Permit2 approval the Router can send only from your own upgraded (EIP-7702) wallet. A payment funded by an organization wallet, or by a wallet that is not upgraded, is refused, and so is its quote. Nothing approved, signed, reserved or charged. Not retryable unchanged: if an organization wallet funds it, pay with an existing delegation funded by your own wallet; otherwise pick a service that charges a fixed price.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0057` (503) — a usage-based (x402 `upto`) payment needs your wallet to approve Permit2 once for the token, which the Router does for you, and that approval did not complete or could not be confirmed: the bundler, paymaster, chain RPC or Privy failed, the transaction reverted, or a failure in the last 5 minutes is still in its backoff window (a retry inside it gets `0057` again). No payment signed, reserved or charged. Retry after 5 minutes with the same `requestId`; report it if it persists.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0058` (422) — a usage-based (x402 `upto`) call's routing fee is taken later, on what the merchant settles, in one transfer with your other fees on that network, and this deployment does not collect fees that way on the service's network; its quote is refused the same way. Nothing approved, signed, reserved or charged. Not retryable: a configuration gap on our side; pick a fixed-price service (or one on another network), or report it.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0059` (400) — `POST /router/run`: a forwarded `Accept` names `text/event-stream`, a stream `/run` cannot relay. Nothing sent to the service or charged. Stream via `/router/svc/<slug>` (credits, and any commerce grant: `/router/subscription/svc/<slug>`), or drop that Accept.
- `BCK.ROUTER.0060` (403) — the service is a partner API Nevermined resells, sold for Nevermined credits only: no delegation pays it, by slug or by its URL. Nothing sent to the service or charged. Pay it with credits (`/router/subscription/route`, `/router/subscription/svc/<slug>`, or `/router/run` with credits).
- `BCK.ROUTER.0061` (500) — a credits call to a partner service: after its payment was recorded, the Router could not sign the partner-edge token its paid hop carries (a configuration or signing fault on our side). The payment named by `params.paymentId` is `Failed` and not delivered, and its credits are refunded (at once, or by the refund engine shortly after); nothing was sent to the service. Retry only once the operator has fixed it, and with a **new** `requestId`; never loop unchanged. Report it with the `correlationId` and the `paymentId`.
- `BCK.X402.0052` (503) — your account wallet's EIP-7702 upgrade, which the Router runs before any payment whichever delegation pays (only where EIP-7702 is enabled), did not complete. Nothing was paid. Usually transient: retry with the same `requestId` after a backoff of a few minutes (a retry inside the fail-closed window that follows a failed upgrade gets `BCK.X402.0052` again); report it if it persists across several such retries.
- `BCK.USER_PROFILE.0006` (503) — Privy or the chain RPC did not answer while the Router checked that the wallet belongs to your account. Nothing signed, reserved or charged. Retry after a short backoff with the same `requestId`.

**Never widen a Delegation, and never create a second one, to get past a refusal.** The cap is the
user's decision, not a runtime obstacle; minting a fresh Delegation to escape an exhausted one
defeats the whole mechanism. Report and stop.

## Accounting

`GET /api/v1/router/payments` (filters `delegationId`, `from`, `to`, `format=csv`; `requestId=<id>` is an exact lookup answering `{ presence, payments }`) and
`/payments/summary`. `amount` is the **merchant leg only**, in the asset's smallest unit — and ⚠️ the
**scale differs per rail**: 6 decimals on the crypto rails, but the card rail (`network: "stripe"`)
is **scale 2, so its `amount` IS cents**. Read `assetDecimals` off the row, never assume 6; `null`
there means unrecognised, so show raw units — and guard that branch, because
`amount / 10 ** null` silently returns the raw atomic amount as a number. `assetSymbol` is echoed even when unrecognised, so it
is not a recognition check, and `pathUSD`/`PathUSD` differ in case across the two Tempo chains —
compare tickers case-insensitively. Rows also carry `feeAtomic`, `feeBps`, `feeCents`, `feeStatus`,
`feeTxHash`, `feeNonce`, `feeFailureReason`. That last one says why the fee did not collect — or, on
a `feeStatus: "None"` row, `fee-not-quoted: <why>` when a rate was configured and none was quoted at
all. Human-facing diagnostic text, never a contract: branch on `feeStatus`.
`feeStatus` (`None|Accrued|Submitted|Settled|Failed|Released`) is a **separate lifecycle** from the
payment `status`, which shares `Settled`/`Failed` — never read one for the other. A record at
`Issued` is **not** an error — the money moved; do not retry it.
